<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crimea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Crimean peninsula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Crimea is a peninsula on the northern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. It was ruled by a succession of steppe powers and later by the Crimean Khanate, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from the fifteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed Crimea in 1783 and fought the Crimean War there in the 1850s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crimea.jpg" alt="Crimea" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Emirr, Spesh531 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crimea_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The plains of eastern <b>Crimea</b> and the Kuban produced wheat and barley in surplus, much of it grown by native farmers paying tribute, and the kings shipped it through Panticapaeum and Theodosia to the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With Lithuanian support and the backing of the leading Crimean Tatar clans, he established an independent khanate in the <b>Crimean peninsula</b> and the neighbouring steppe around 1441; the exact date is uncertain, and some historians prefer 1443 or 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1282 John II married Eudokia, daughter of Michael VIII Palaiologos, and adopted a more modest title, Emperor and Autocrat of all the East, the Iberians and the Perateia, the last term referring to dependencies across the Black Sea in the <b>Crimea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By 576 CE, Göktürk forces had reached the <b>Crimea</b> and were besieging the Byzantine city of Bosporus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pastoral nomadism remained the basis of life on the steppe, with the khan's camp migrating seasonally along the Volga, but the khanate also ruled settled regions: the towns and farmland of Volga Bulgaria, Khwarazm with its city of Urgench, the <b>Crimea</b>, and the tribute-paying principalities of Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Furs, wax, honey and leather were the main exports, traded through Novgorod to the Hanseatic League, down the Volga to Kazan and the steppe, and south towards the <b>Crimea</b>, where Moscow's merchants known as surozhane dealt in silks and other luxuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
